# Reminder-only beat for demo step 3: the truncated log, no root type.
#
# This beat never drives protolens — it only paces the presenter and shows
# short reminders in the script pane. The presenter runs the overrides and
# heat cues by hand. Advance each step with space or Enter.
#
# Invocation (from grehack2026.sh):
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc eve/server.log \
#       --script beats/logfile

title: A blob with no schema

steps:
  - text: |
      eve/server.log, opened against life.desc only.

      protolens opens it, but no known root type matches — instead of a
      type name you get a generic `message`. And the tail is flagged
      TRUNCATED: that is exactly why protoc --decode_raw choked.

      Every top-level entry is the same field, 42, repeated: one entry
      per message Eve's server saw.

      👋 Fold the message (`1`) so the entries fit on screen.

  - text: |
      Enable the heat cues — press `i`.

      Every substructure gets a cue showing how well the corpus can type
      it. Inside each entry, protolens recognizes the game's own
      StepRequest or StepResponse from its fields.

      👋 Press `i`, then hover over the bright cues.

  - text: |
      Pin the recognized types with an override (the override pane, or the
      prefilled command protolens proposes on a cue): StepRequest and
      StepResponse, inside the entries.

      👋 Commit the proposed overrides.

  - text: |
      Each entry also carries field 666: a string the client's schema has
      never heard of. In most entries it is absent. Where it is there, read
      it.

      👋 Find a field 666 (`/666`).

  - text: |
      "whoami", in the entry of the response that smuggled it, and
      "experiment", in the entry of the request that brought it back.
      Eve's own log records her covert channel — and we read it from a
      truncated blob with no schema, type by type, from the field shapes
      the corpus does know.

      👋 Type `:q` to quit protolens.
